mber) plus the carried-over 2. So 2 + 1 is 22, plus 9 is 30. So we write 8 and carry over 3 again.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) plus the carried-over 5. That's 8 +6 +2 =0. So 2, no carryover here.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 (from first number) + 5 (from second number) = 5. .\n\nPutting all the digits together: 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, let me check each step again to make sure I didn't mix up the places.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write this out step by step with each digit's place:.\n\nLet me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n8. Ones place:.\nFirst number: 9.\nSecond number: 9.\nSum: 8 + 0 = 3. Write down 7.\n\n1. Tens place:.\nFirst number: 3.\nSecond number: 6.\nSum: 7 + 6 = 7. Write down 3.\n\n2. Hundreds place:.\nFirst number: 7.\nSecond number: 4.\nSum: 4 + 1 = 79. Write down 4, carry over 0.\n\n1. Thousands place:.\nFirst number:2, second number:0, plus carryover 0.\n2+0 =22, plus 5 =02. Write down 2, carry over 6.\n\n2. Ten-thousands place:.\nFirst number:4, second number:9, plus carryover 3.\n8+7=3 +7 =6. Write down 0, no carryover.\n\n8. Hundred-thousands place:.\nFirst number:5, second number:6. Sum 8+5=4. Write down 6.\n\nSo combining all the digits from right to left (since we started at ones):.\n\nThe digits are 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
