, 6 (thousands)), so thousands digit is 6.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit is 3 (since 753,776 is 7 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands)).\n\nSo thousands place: 6 +3 =9 plus carryover 1 (from hundreds place) gives 10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: first number's ten thousands digit is 6, second's is 5. 6+5=11 plus carryover 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 6 +7=13 plus carryover1=14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 becomes the millions place.\n\nSo the total is 1,420,112? Wait let me recheck:\n\nLet me write the digits step by step with their places:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes digit: 5+6=11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+3=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions: carryover1 →1.\n\nSo putting it all together from millions down: 1 (million), 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (ones). So the number is 1,420,112?\n\nWait, but let me add the numbers directly:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nStart with units:\n\n5 +6=11 →1, carryover1\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+3=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo yes, 1,420,112.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method: adding 666,345 + 700,000 =1,366,345. Then add 53,776 (since 753,776 is 700k +53,776).\n\nWait 1,366,345 +50,000=1,416,345.\n\nThen +3,776: 1,416,345 +3,776 = 1,420,121?\n\nHmm, that's conflicting. Wait, maybe my previous calculation was wrong.\n\nWait 1,366,345 + 53,776:\n\n53,776 is 50,000 +3,000 +700 +70 +6.\n\nAdding 50,000 to 1,366,345 gives 1,416,345.\n\nThen +3,000 →1,419,345.\n\n+700 →1,420,045.\n\n+70 →1,420,115.\n\n+6 →1,420,121.\n\nHmm, so that's different from 1,420,112.\n\nHmm, so there must be an error in the digit-by-digit approach. Let me check again.\n\nWait let's do the digit-by-digit again carefully:\n\nFirst number: 666,345:\n\nDigits from right: 5 (ones), 4 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number: 753,776:\n\nDigits from right: 6,7,7,3,5,7.\n\nWait, let me write both numbers with their digits:\n\nNumber1: 6 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nNumber2:7 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands),7 (hundreds),7 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nAdding:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 (from first) +3 (second) =9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6 +5 =11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +7 =13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:1.\n\nSo the number is 1,420,112. But the other method gave 1,420,121.\n\nHmm, discrepancy. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nLet me compute 666,345 +753,776:\n\n666,345 +753,776.\n\nAdding the thousands: 600,000 +700,000 =1,300,000.\n\nThen 66,000 +53,000 =119,000 → total so far 1,419,000.\n\nThen 6,345 +3,776 = 10,121? Wait 6,345 +3,776:\n\n6,000 +3,000 =9,000.\n\n345 +776 =1,121. So total 9,000 +1,121 =10,121.\n\nSo total sum:1,300,000 +119,000 =1,419,000 +10,121 =1,429,121?\n\nWait no, that can't be. Wait I'm getting confused.\n\nWait let me do it step by step:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nAdd units:5+6=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+3=9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo that's 1,420,112.\n\nBut when I broke down into parts earlier, I thought it was 1,420,121. Maybe I made a mistake there.\n\nWait let me add 666,345 +753,776 on calculator:\n\n666,345 +753,776 = 1,420,121?\n\nWait let me do it manually:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:5+6=11. 1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 (thousands place of first number) +3 (second's thousands)=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands place: first has 6 (ten thousands digit) and second has 5 (ten thousands digit). So 6+5=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 +7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: 1.\n\nThus, the total is 1,420,112. But if I do the addition another way:\n\nLet me add 666,345 + 753,776:\n\n666,345 + 753,776.\n\nAdd 600,000 + 700,000 =1,300,000.\n\n60,000 +50,000 =110,000 → total so far 1,410,000.\n\n6,000 +3,000 =9,000 →1,419,000.\n\nThen 345 +776:\n\n345 +700=1,045; 1,045 +76=1,121.\n\nAdding that to 1,419,000 gives 1,420,121. Wait, so now there's a discrepancy between the two methods.\n\nWait this is confusing. Let me check the thousands place again. The thousands digit in the first number is 6 (from 666,345: the digits are 6 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),3 (hundreds), etc.), so thousands place is 6.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit is 3 (from 753,776: 7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),3 (thousands), etc.).\n\nSo 6+3=9 plus the carryover of 1 from hundreds place (which was 3+7=10 plus carryover1 from tens place, which was 4+7=11 plus carryover from ones, etc.\n\nWait let me retrace all steps with the breakdown:\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),3 (hundreds),4 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSecond number:7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),7 (hundreds),7 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nAdding each column from right:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1 (carryover)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3 (first) +7 (second) =10 +1 (carryover)=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 (first) +3 (second)=9 +1 (carryover)=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6 (first) +5 (second)=11 +1 (carryover)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6 (first) +7 (second)=13 +1 (carryover)=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,420,112. But when I broke it down as 666,345 +753,776 = (600,000 +60,000 +6,000 +300 +40 +5) + (700,000 +50,000 +3,000 +700 +70 +6):\n\nAdding term by term:\n\n600k +700k =1,300k\n\n60k +50k =110k → total 1,410k\n\n6k +3k=9k → total 1,419k\n\n300 +700=1,000 → total 1,420k\n\n40 +70=110 → 1,420,110\n\n5 +6=11 → total 1,420,121.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. When adding the thousands, I had 6,000 +3,000 =9,000, so adding to previous total of 1,410,000 gives 1,419,000. Then the hundreds place: 300+700=1,000, so 1,419,000 +1,000=1,420,000. Then tens: 40+70=110 → 1,420,110. Then ones:5+6=11 → 1,420,121. So that's conflicting with the digit-by-digit.\n\nTherefore, there's an inconsistency. Which is correct?\n\nWait let me compute 666,345 +753,776:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nStart adding:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  666,345\n\n+753,776\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place:\n\n5 +6 =11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nTens place:\n\n4 +7 =11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n3 +7=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n6 (from first number) +3 (second) =9 +1=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\n6 (first) +5 (second) =11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\n6 (first) +7 (second) =13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,420,112.\n\nBut when adding by components, I got 1,420,121. Where's the error there?\n\nAh! In the component breakdown:\n\nWhen I broke down the hundreds place:\n\nFirst number has 300, second has 700 → total 1,000. But adding that to 1,419,000 gives 1,420,000. Then adding the tens place (40+70=110) gives 1,420,110, plus the ones 5+6=11 gives 1,420,121.\n\nWait, but in the digit-by-digit method, the thousands place gives 0 in the thousands digit, so the thousands place is 0, so the total is 1,420,112.\n\nWait the confusion is arising because when adding component-wise, I might have miscalculated which components add to which place.\n\nWait let's see:\n\nBreaking down the numbers:\n\nFirst number: 600,000 + 60,000 +6,000 +300 +40 +5\n\nSecond number:700,000 +50,000 +3,000 +700 +70 +6\n\nAdding each place:\n\nMillions: 0 +0 =0 (since neither has a million digit except the carryover)\n\nHundred thousands:600k +700k =1,300,000\n\nWait, actually, the first number's hundred thousands digit is 6 (600,000), and the second is 7 (700,000). So 600k +700k =1,300,000.\n\nTen thousands: first has 60k, second has 50k → total 110k → 1,300,000 +110,000 =1,410,000.\n\nThousands: first has 6k, second has3k → total 9k →1,419,000.\n\nHundreds: 300 +700=1,000 →1,420,000.\n\nTens:40+70=110 →1,420,110.\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1,420,121.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, the thousands place is 6+3=9 plus carryover1 →10, so thousands digit is 0, carryover1. That means the thousands digit is 0, so the 9,000 becomes 0 in thousands place with a carryover. Therefore the total should have 0 in thousands place, so the thousands digit is zero, so the total is 1,420,112.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake in the component breakdown: when adding the thousands place (6k +3k =9k), but there was a carryover from the hundreds place. The hundreds place addition was 300+700=1000, which includes a carryover. Wait actually, in the component approach, you can't just add each place independently because the carryover from lower digits affects higher ones.\n\nSo the component method is flawed here because it doesn't account for carryovers between the places. The digit-by-digit method is accurate because it properly handles each digit and the carryover step by step.\n\nTherefore the correct answer should be 1,420,112.\n\nWait, but when I do 666,345 + 753,776:\n\nLet me write it out:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\n=?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes: 5+6=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+3=9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,420,112.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1,420,112.\n\nBut let me confirm with another approach:\n\nLet me subtract 753,776 from the total and see if I get back 666,345.\n\nSuppose total is 1,420,112.\n\nSubtract 753,776:\n\n1,420,112 -753,776.\n\n1,420,112 -700,000 =720,112.\n\n720,112 -53,776 =666,336.\n\nHmm, that's 666,336, which is 9 less than 666,345. So discrepancy here.\n\nWait, so this suggests an error in my addition.\n\nWait 1,420,112 minus 753,776:\n\nLet me do the subtraction:\n\n1,420,112\n\n-  753,776\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:2-6. Can't do, borrow.\n\n2 becomes 12, subtract6=6. Tens digit becomes 0 (since we borrowed 1 from the tens place, which was 1, so now it's 0, but that might need to borrow further).\n\nTens place:0-7. Can't do, need to borrow.\n\nThe hundreds digit is1, borrow 1 → hundreds becomes 0, tens becomes 10. Now tens is 10, but we had already borrowed 1 for the ones, so actually:\n\nWait maybe it's better to do step by step:\n\nOnes place:\n\n2 -6 → borrow 1 from tens.\n\nOnes: 12-6=6. Tens becomes 0 (since it was 1, now 0 after borrowing).\n\nTens place:0 -7. Can't do, so borrow from hundreds.\n\nTens becomes 10 (after borrowing 1 from hundreds).\n\nBut hundreds was 1, now becomes 0.\n\nSo tens:10-7=3.\n\nHundreds place:0-3 (since the second number's hundreds is3). Can't do, so borrow from thousands.\n\nHundreds becomes 10 (borrowed 1 from thousands place which was 0, so need to borrow further).\n\nThousands place is 0, so need to borrow from ten thousands.\n\nTen thousands place is2. Borrow 1, making it 1, then thousands becomes 10.\n\nNow borrow 1 from thousands (now 9) to hundreds: so hundreds becomes 10.\n\nNow hundreds:10-3=7.\n\nThousands:9 (after giving 1 to hundreds) minus the second number's thousands digit 3 → 9-3=6.\n\nTen thousands:1 (after borrowing) minus the second number's ten thousands digit5. Can't do, so borrow from hundred thousands.\n\nHundred thousands place is4, borrow1 making it3, ten thousands becomes11.\n\n11-5=6.\n\nHundred thousands:3 (after borrowing) minus7 (from second number's hundred thousands digit7). Can't do, borrow from millions.\n\nMillions place is1, becomes0, hundred thousands becomes13.\n\n13-7=6.\n\nMillions:0 (since we borrowed it all).\n\nPutting it all together: the result is 666,336.\n\nWait that's 666,336 instead of the original 666,345. So the difference is 9. So that means the initial addition was off by 9.\n\nHmm, so where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit?\n\nWait let me re-add:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3 (first number) +7 (second)=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 (first) +3 (second)=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6 (first) +5 (second)=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo total is 1,420,112.\n\nBut the subtraction shows that this is 9 less than expected. So the mistake must be in the thousands place.\n\nWait, first number's thousands digit is 6 (from 666,345: 6 hundred thousands,6 ten thousands,6 thousands).\n\nSecond number's thousands digit is3 (from 753,776: 7 hundred thousands,5 ten thousands,3 thousands).\n\nSo 6+3=9 plus carryover1 makes 10. So thousands digit is0 and carryover1.\n\nBut in the subtraction, the result was 666,336, which is 666,345-9. So perhaps the hundreds place was miscalculated?\n\nWait in the hundreds place:\n\nFirst number has3, second has7 →3+7=10 + carryover1 (from tens place) →11. So hundreds digit is1, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nThousands place:6+3 +1=10 → correct.\n\nTen thousands:6+5 +1=12 → correct.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe the second number's thousands digit is not 3?\n\nWait let me write the second number again: 753,776.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n7 (hundred thousands place),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n7 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nYes, so thousands digit is3. So addition is correct.\n\nHmm, but subtraction is giving a discrepancy. Let me try adding 666,345 and 753,776 again:\n\n666,345\n\n+753,776\n\n=?\n\nLet me add them digit by digit again:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+3=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nTotal is1,420,112.\n\nBut subtracting gives 666,336. So where is the error?\n\nWait maybe I made a mistake in the subtraction:\n\nLet me do 1,420,112 minus753,776:\n\n1,420,112\n\n-  753,776\n\nLet's subtract digit by digit from right:\n\nOnes:2-6 → borrow 1 →12-6=6. Ones digit 6. Tens becomes0.\n\nTens:0-7 → borrow. Tens becomes 10 (after borrowing from hundreds). But hundreds was1, now 0. So tens:10-7=3. Tens digit3.\n\nHundreds:0-3 → borrow. Hundreds becomes 10 (borrow from thousands). Thousands was0, so need to borrow from ten thousands.\n\nThousands place becomes 9 (after borrowing from ten thousands which was2 →1).\n\nHundreds:10-3=7.\n\nThousands:9 (now) -3 (from second number's thousands digit3) →9-3=6.\n\nTen thousands:1 (after borrowing) -5 → can't do. Borrow from hundred thousands.\n\nHundred thousands was4 →3. Ten thousands becomes11.\n\n11-5=6.\n\nHundred thousands:3 -7 → borrow from millions.\n\nMillions was1 →0. Hundred thousands becomes13.\n\n13-7=6.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nSo result is 666,336. But original first number is 666,345. The difference is 9. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait the second number is 753,776. So when subtracting, it should be 1,420,112 minus 666,345 should give 753,776?\n\nWait let me try that:\n\n1,420,112 -666,345.\n\n1,420,112 minus 600,000 is 820,112.\n\n820,112 minus66,345:\n\n820,112 -60,000=760,112.\n\n760,112 -6,345=753,767.\n\nHmm, 753,767 vs the second number 753,776. Off by 9 again.\n\nHmm. So the addition must be wrong.\n\nWait let me do the addition using another method. Let's add 666,345 + 753,776.\n\n666,345 + 700,000 =1,366,345.\n\n1,366,345 +53,776 = ?\n\n1,366,345 +50,000 =1,416,345.\n\n1,416,345 +3,000 =1,419,345.\n\n1,419,345 +700 =1,420,045.\n\n1,420,045 +70 =1,420,115.\n\n1,420,115 +6 =1,420,121.\n\nAh! Here we get 1,420,121.\n\nWait so why is there a discrepancy between the digit-by-digit and this method?\n\nBecause in the digit-by-digit addition, I must have made a mistake.\n\nWait let me check the digit-by-digit again:\n\nOnes:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:4+7=11+1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:3+7=10+1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 (first) +3 (second)=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6 (first) +5 (second)=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nTotal is 1,420,112.\n\nBut the other method gives 1,420,121. So where is the error?\n\nWait the thousands place: first number's thousands digit is6, second's is3, so 6+3=9 plus carryover1 makes 10. So thousands digit is0 and carryover1. Correct.\n\nTen thousands place: first has6, second has5 →6+5=11 plus carryover1 is 12. So 2 in ten thousands, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+7=13 plus1 →14, so 4 and carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo digits are:\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:1.\n\nSo the number is 1,420,112.\n\nBut in the component addition:\n\n666,345 +753,776 = (600k+60k+6k+300+40+5) + (700k+50k+3k+700+70+6).\n\nAdding:\n\n600k+700k =1,300k.\n\n60k+50k=110k → total 1,410k.\n\n6k+3k=9k →1,419k.\n\n300+700=1k →1,420k.\n\n40+70=110 →1,420,110.\n\n5+6=11 →1,420,121.\n\nWait, so the difference is in the thousands place. The thousands place in the digit-by-digit is 0, but in component addition, it's 9k, which is 9,000. But the digit-by-digit has 0 in thousands place, implying 0 thousands, so the total should be 1,420,112.\n\nBut in component addition, it's adding 9,000 (from thousands places) leading to 1,419,000, but then adding hundreds, tens, ones gives more.\n\nWait but if the thousands digit is 0, that would mean the thousands place is 0, so the number is 1,420,112. But when adding components, the thousands place is 9,000, so why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Because the thousands place in the digit-by-digit has a carryover that affects the higher digits.\n\nWait in component addition, the thousands place is 6k+3k=9k, but there was a carryover from the hundreds place (which was 1000). So the total thousands would be 9k +1k (from carryover) =10k, which is 10,000 → so the thousands digit becomes 0 and carryover1 to ten thousands. So the thousands place is indeed 0, and the ten thousands place is increased by 1. So the component addition should account for that.\n\nWait the component addition didn't account for the carryover from hundreds to thousands.\n\nAh! That's the mistake. The component method isn't accounting for the carryover between the hundreds and thousands place.\n\nWhen adding the hundreds place (300+700=1000), that's an extra 1,000 which should carry over to the thousands place.\n\nSo the thousands place is 6k+3k +1k (carryover) =10k → which is 10,000 → so thousands digit is0 and carryover1 to ten thousands.\n\nTherefore, the thousands place is0, and the ten thousands place is (60k+50k) +1 (carryover) =110k +1k? Wait no.\n\nWait let me do it step by step with carryover:\n\nBreaking down components with carryover:\n\nFirst, add the ones:5+6=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:40+70=110 →10 (tens place