umber's thousands place is 9. Wait, no. Wait, the thousands digit in the second number: Let's see. 837,824: the digits are 0 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, yes, so the thousands place is 8. So adding the thousands digits: first number's thousands is 3, second is 0. So 1 + 0 = 85, plus the carried over 3 from the hundreds place makes 71. So we write 1 in the thousands place and carry over 2 again.\n\nNext, the ten thousands place. First number has 6, second has 3. 0 + 5 = 06, plus the carried 3 is 84. So put 7 in ten thousands and carry over 6.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: first number has 6, second has 6. 4 +1 =61, plus the 3 carried over is 57. So we write 2 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 3. Since there are no more digits, that 0 becomes the next digit (millions place). So putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 9.\n\nTens: 0 (with carryover 3).\n\nHundreds: 9 (carryover 7).\n\nThousands: 4 (carryover 5).\n\nTen thousands: 8 (carryover 3).\n\nHundred thousands: 6 (carryover 9).\n\nMillions: 0.\n\nSo the total sum is 5,623,250?.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should write each step properly.\n\nLet me write each digit:.\n\nFirst number: 6 1 3 8 9 3.\n\nSecond number:2 5 2 8 1 9.\n\nWait, actually, when written vertically:.\n\n      6 0 1 5 7 9.\n\n    + 4 9 2 5 6 5.\n\n    ----------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n4 +8 =3. Write 5.\n\nTens: 4 +5 =69. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds:2 +3 =34, plus 4 is 28. Write 6, carryover 4? Wait, wait, no, wait, the first number's hundreds digit is 6 (third digit from the right?), let me see:.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to clarify the positions properly. Let me list the digits with their place values.\n\nFirst number: 146,649:.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\nDigit 9 (ones): 8.\n\nDigit 1 (tens):8.\n\nDigit 7 (hundreds):6.\n\nDigi