      "text": "786,543. Wait, let me check again because I might have messed up some of the carries. Maybe I should write it out vertically properly.\n\nWait, hold on. The numbers are actually 336,695 and 349,953. Let me correct that. Let me line them up:\n\n  336695\n+349953\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 5 + 3 = 8. Write down 8.\n\nTens place: 9 + 5 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 9 = 15, plus the 1 carried over is 16. Write 6, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 + 9 = 12, plus 1 is 13. Write 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 4 = 7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 3 = 6.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum should be 686,648? Wait, let me add again step by step:\n\nLet me do each digit carefully:\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n       3 3 6 6 9 5\n     + 3 4 9 9 5 3\n     ----------------\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n5 + 3 = 8 \u2192 8\n\nNext, tens place: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 9 = 15 + 1 (carry) = 16 \u2192 6, carry 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 (from first number) + 9 (second number) = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 (first) + 4 (second) = 7 + 1 (carry) = 8 \u2192 8.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 3 = 6 \u2192 6.\n\nSo combining all digits: 6 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds?), wait:\n\nWait the positions are:\n\nEach digit from right:\n\nOnes: 8\n\nTens:4\n\nHundreds:6\n\nThousands:3\n\nTen-thousands:8\n\nHundred-thousands:6\n\nSo the number is 686,648?\n\nWait, let's count the digits:\n\nHundred-thousands is the 6, then ten-thousands 8, thousands place is 6, hundreds place 6, tens 4, ones 8. Wait that can't be right. Wait let me structure it properly:\n\nThe digits from right to left:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6\n\nSo the number is 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds?), wait no:\n\nWait the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let me write it as:\n\nNumber: 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 6 (tens?), no:\n\nWait let's write the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the left, the first digit is hundred thousands place:\n\n6 (hundred thousands), then 8 (ten thousands), then 6 (thousands), then 3 (hundreds), then 6 (tens?), no, wait:\n\nWait the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let me see:\n\nIf the sum is:\n\nHundred thousands: 6\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:8\n\nWait that would be 686,348?\n\nWait I think I messed up the thousands and ten thousands place. Let me recheck.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n336,695 is:\n\n3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\n349,953 is:\n\n3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nAdding them:\n\nHundred thousands: 3+3=6\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7\n\nThousands:6+9=15 \u2192 5 with carryover 1?\n\nWait no, I think my earlier step-by-step was wrong. Let me start over properly.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n     3 3 6 6 9 5\n   + 3 4 9 9 5 3\n   ----------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nOnes: 5 + 3 = 8. Write 8.\n\nTens: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:6 + 9 = 15 + 1 (carry) = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 (from first number's thousands place) + 9 (second number's thousands) = 12 + 1 (carry) = 13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 (first number's ten thousands) + 4 (second's) = 7 + 1 (carry) = 8. Write 8.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 3 + 3 =6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred-thousands:6,\n\nTen-thousands:8,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nWait, but the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let me structure the number correctly.\n\nThe digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:8,\n\nTens:4,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nTen-thousands:8,\n\nHundred-thousands:6.\n\nSo the number is 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands?), wait no. Wait the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right, so the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):8\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6\n\nSo the number is 686,348?\n\nWait, let's write it out:\n\nStarting from the highest place:\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nWait, that would be 683,648?\n\nWait no, the thousands place is the fourth digit. Let's write it as:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nWait, so the number is 683,648?\n\nWait that doesn't seem right. Let me try adding the numbers another way. Let's convert them to expanded form:\n\n336,695 = 300,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 + 600 + 90 +5\n\n349,953 = 300,000 +40,000 +9,000 +900 +50 +3\n\nAdding each place:\n\n300,000 +300,000 =600,000\n\n30,000 +40,000 =70,000\n\n6,000 +9,000 =15,000\n\n600 +900 =1,500\n\n90 +50=140\n\n5 +3=8\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n600,000 +70,000 =670,000\n\n670,000 +15,000 =685,000\n\n685,000 +1,500 =686,500\n\n686,500 +140 =686,640\n\n686,640 +8 =686,648\n\nAh! So the correct sum is 686,648.\n\nWait so earlier when I did the digit-by-digit, I must have made a mistake in the thousands place. Let me check that again.\n\nIn the thousands place:\n\nFirst number's thousands digit: Looking at 336,695: the digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones). So thousands place is 6.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit in 349,953 is the thousands place: the digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones)