ht to left are:\n\nOnes:8,\n\nTens:4,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nTen-thousands:8,\n\nHundred-thousands:6.\n\nSo the number is 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands?), wait no. Wait the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right, so the digits are:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):8\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6\n\nSo the number is 686,348?\n\nWait, let's write it out:\n\nStarting from the highest place:\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nWait, that would be 683,648?\n\nWait no, the thousands place is the fourth digit. Let's write it as:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nWait, so the number is 683,648?\n\nWait that doesn't seem right. Let me try adding the numbers another way. Let's convert them to expanded form:\n\n336,695 = 300,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 + 600 + 90 +5\n\n349,953 = 300,000 +40,000 +9,000 +900 +50 +3\n\nAdding each place:\n\n300,000 +300,000 =600,000\n\n30,000 +40,000 =70,000\n\n6,000 +9,000 =15,000\n\n600 +900 =1,500\n\n90 +50=140\n\n5 +3=8\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n600,000 +70,000 =670,000\n\n670,000 +15,000 =685,000\n\n685,000 +1,500 =686,500\n\n686,500 +140 =686,640\n\n686,640 +8 =686,648\n\nAh! So the correct sum is 686,648.\n\nWait so earlier when I did the digit-by-digit, I must have made a mistake in the thousands place. Let me check that again.\n\nIn the thousands place:\n\nFirst number's thousands digit: Looking at 336,695: the digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 5 (ones). So thousands place is 6.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit in 349,953 is the thousands place: the digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (ones). So thousands digit is 9.\n\nSo 6 +9 =15. But wait, in the thousands place addition, there was a carryover from the hundreds place?\n\nWait let's go through the digit-by-digit again carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes:5+3=8 (no carryover)\n\nTens:9+5=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:6+9=15 +1 (carryover) =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 6 (first number's thousands) +9 (second's thousands) =15 +1 (carryover from hundreds) =16. So here, thousands place would be 6, carryover 1.\n\nWait, so thousands digit: 6+9=15, plus the 1 makes 16. So we write 6 in thousands place, carryover 1 to the next (ten thousands place).\n\nThen, ten thousands place: 3 (first number's ten thousands) +4 (second's) =7 +1 (carryover) =8. So ten thousands is 8, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands: 3+3=6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nOnes:8,\n\nTens:4,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nThousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nWait that makes the number 686,648. Which matches the expanded form total.\n\nEarlier I must have miscounted the thousands place. So the correct answer is 686,648.\n\nTherefore, the final computed sum is 686,648.\n\nWait but in my first digit-by-digit I thought the thousands digit was 3, but that was a mistake. The thousands digit in the first number is 6 (from 336,695: the third digit from the left is 6, which is the thousands place?), no, let's clarify the place values:\n\nLet's take 336,695:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (thousands)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n5 (ones)\n\nYes. So the thousands digit is the third from the left (the 6). The second number, 349,953:\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n9 (thousands)\n\n9 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n3 (ones)\n\nSo thousands digit is 9. So 6+9=15, plus the carryover 1 makes 16. So thousands digit is 6, carryover 1 to ten thousands. So the thousands place is 6, not 3 as I initially thought. That was my mistake earlier.\n\nSo the correct digits are:\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:8