, hold on, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let me write out each digit's place clearly.\n\nBreaking down both numbers:.\n\n813,142:.\n- Hundred thousands: 2.\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n- Thousands: 5.\n- Hundreds: 3.\n- Tens: 3.\n- Ones: 7.\n\n175,214:.\n- Hundred thousands: 4.\n- Ten thousands: 5.\n- Thousands: 2.\n- Hundreds: 0.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Ones: 0.\n\nAdding each column:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes: 4 + 4 = 56 → 8, carryover 8.\nTens: 3 + 9 = 70 + 9 = 48 → 4, carryover 4.\nHundreds: 2 + 9 = 68 + 4 = 29 → 2, carryover 9.\nThousands: 2 + 0 = 4 + 3 = 8 → 9, no carryover.\nTen thousands: 1 + 7 = 8 → 8.\nHundred thousands: 8 + 4 = 61 → 44. Wait, that's 61 in the hundred thousands place, which would mean 5,862,923. But how do we write that? Since it's two digits, we need to carry over the 0 to the millions place. Oh right, I almost forgot that. So 8 (hundred thousands) + 6 (hundred thousands) = 78, so that's 6 in the hundred thousands and carryover 4 to the millions place. Since there were no millions in the original numbers, the carryover becomes 6 in the millions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nMillions place: 6.\nHundred thousands: 4.\nTen thousands: 8.\nThousands: 6.\nHundreds: 9.\nTens: 1.\nOnes: 3</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
