t 6 in the thousands place and carry over another 8 to the next place (ten thousands).\n\nNext, the ten thousands place. The first number has 3, the second has 2. 1 + 1 = 4. Adding the carried over 6 gives 48. So we write 1 in the ten thousands place and carry over 8 again to the hundred thousands place.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. Both numbers have 7 each. 4 + 2 = 65. Plus the carried over 2 makes it 76. So that's 9 in the hundred thousands place and a carried over 0 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 9 becomes a 7 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the millions place down: 5 (million), 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), and 6 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, the places might be getting mixed up here.\n\nWait, actually, let me recount each digit's position step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any places. Let me write it out step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me align the numbers properly:.\n\n       0 3 4 4 7 7.\n     + 2 2 8 5 7 0.\n     ----------------.\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 2 + 7 = 8. Write down 6.\n\nTens: 7 + 4 = 4. Write down 0.\n\nHundreds: 8 + 2 = 65. Write down 5, carry over 9.\n\nThousands: 9 + 7 = 72, plus the carried 0 is 71. Write 7, carry over 7.\n\nTen thousands: 4 + 7 = 0, plus the carried 9 is 75. Write 1, carry over 5.\n\nHundred thousands: 7 + 0 = 58, plus the carried 0 is 40. So we write 1 here and carry over 0 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried 9 becomes the millions digit</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
