 (from first number's hundreds place) + 4 (second's hundreds) = 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:\n9 (first number's thousands) + 4 (second's thousands) = 13, plus carryover 1 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:\n7 (first's ten-thousands) + 3 (second's ten-thousands) = 10, plus carryover 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands:\n6 (first's hundred-thousands) + 3 (second's hundred-thousands) = 9, plus carryover 1 = 10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: The carryover 1.\n\nPutting all digits together from right to left:\n\nMillions: 1, Hundred-thousands: 0, Ten-thousands: 1, Thousands: 4, Hundreds:1, Tens:2, Ones:1.\n\nSo the total is 1,024,121? Wait, that can't be right. Let me add them numerically:\n\n679,715 + 344,406.\n\nLet me do it another way to verify:\n\n679,715 + 300,000 = 979,715\n\n979,715 + 44,406 = 1,024,121. Okay, that matches. So the correct answer is 1,024,121. But let me check the digit-by-digit again step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 5 + 6 = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 1 + 0 = 1 + 1 (carry) = 2. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 7 + 4 = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 9 + 4 = 13 + 1 =14 → 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 3 = 10 + 1 (carry) = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nThen the million place is 1. So combining all digits:\n\n1 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 1 (hundred), 2 (ten), 1 (one). So 1,024,121. Yes, that's correct.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm with another method. Let's add 679,715 + 344,406:\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n600,000 + 300,000 = 900,000\n\n79,715 + 44,406 = ?\n\n79,715 + 40,000 = 119,715\n\n119,715 + 4,406 = 124,121\n\nAdding to the 900,000 gives 1,024,121. Yep. So the correct answer is 1,024,121. I think my initial confusion was due to misreading the numbers initially, but now it's clear.\n\nWait, but in the problem statement, the user had:\n\nThe question is 679715 and 344406. So