ace (ten-thousands place).\n\nNext is the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 4, and the second has a 0. Adding the carried-over 0 gives 9 + 3 + 6 = 08. Again, that's 02. So we write 3 in the ten-thousands place and carry over 9 to the hundred-thousands place.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 0. Adding the carried-over 2 gives 0 + 6 + 9 = 20. So we write 9 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 0 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits left, that carried-over 7 becomes the millions place digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from right to left (starting from ones) are:.\n\nOnes: 2.\n\nTens: 3.\n\nHundreds: 3.\n\nThousands: 0.\n\nTen-thousands: 6.\n\nHundred-thousands: 2.\n\nMillions: 2.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, when we added the hundred-thousands place (8 + 0 + 3), that's 44, so the hundred-thousands digit is 3 and carryover 8 to millions. So the millions digit is 8</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
