et me recount each step carefully. Let me write each digit's place:.\n\nLet's break down each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 303,829.\nBreaking down:.\n- Hundred thousands: 5.\n- Ten thousands:1.\n- Thousands:3.\n- Hundreds:2.\n- Tens:6.\n- Ones:6.\n\nSecond number:358,781.\nBreaking down:.\n- Hundred thousands:3.\n- Ten thousands:5.\n- Thousands:7.\n- Hundreds:6.\n- Tens:7.\n- Ones:6.\n\nNow adding each column starting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes place: 3 +9 =5. Write down 3.\n\nTens place:9 +8=04. Write 2, carryover 7.\n\nHundreds place:0 +0=95, plus the carried over 2 makes 32. Write 5, carryover 9.\n\nThousands place:6 (from first number) +8 (second number) =29, plus 4 carried over is 38. Write 4, carryover 6.\n\nWait, hold on, the thousands place in the first number is 6? Wait let me confirm:.\n\nWait, the first number is 468,022. Let me parse each digit:.\n\nBreaking 640,363:.\n\nPositions from right (ones to hundred thousands):.\n\nPosition 4 (ones):6.\n\nPosition 0 (tens):2.\n\nPosition 1 (hundreds):1.\n\nPosition 5 (thousands):2.\n\nPosition 9 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition 8 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nWait a second, maybe I miscounted earlier. Let me write them again properly.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising from how the digits are separated. Let me write each number as individual digits:.\n\nFirst number: 0 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSecond number:3 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +7.\n\nTen thousands:1 +4.\n\nThousands:8 +0.\n\nHundreds:5 +9.\n\nTens:2 +0.\n\nOnes:0 +3.\n\nWait, but addition is done from the ones to the higher places. Let me start over:.\n\nStarting from the ones digit:.\n\nOnes:9 +1 =4. No carryover. So first digit is 2.\n\nTens:0 +3 =03. So write 4, carryover 7.\n\nHundreds:2 +7 =69, plus 4 carried over =57. Write 1, carryover 4.\n\nThousands:7 +4 =90, plus 9 carried over =80. Write 8, carryover 4.\n\nTen thousands:2 +2 =77, plus 8 carried over =33. Write 6, carryover 7.\n\nHundred thousands:7 +8 =62, plus 7 carried over =67. So write 8 and carryover 4. But since there are no more digits, that 5 is added as a new digit to the left.\n\nWait, that would make the total digits 8. Let me see:.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nHundred thousands:3 (from 09+2 carried over, but actually 67, so 7 in the hundred thousands and carryover 5 to millions place?).\n\nWait, let me track each step again step by step carefully.\n\nLet me write it vertically:.\n\n          7 9 0 4 4 6.\n        + 3 7 1 4 0 8.\n        ---------------.\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes:7 +8 =9. Write 0. No carryover.\n\nTens place:6 +3 =35. Write 4, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds place:7 +4 =63, plus the 7 carried over is 40. Write 5, carryover 4.\n\nThousands place:8 (from first number) +7 (second number) =89, plus 2 carried over is 41. Write 0, carryover 6.\n\nTen thousands place:2 +8 =76, plus 7 carried over =33. Write 8, carryover 5.\n\nHundred thousands place:4 +3 =96, plus 8 carried over =78. Here, we can't write 28 in a single digit, so we write 3 and carryover 2 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried over 6 becomes a new digit at the millions place.\n\nSo putting all together, the sum is:.\n\n2 (millions) 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write it step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each column with the carryovers:.\n\n- Ones:0.\n\n- Tens:2 (with carryover 9).\n\n- Hundreds:3 (carryover 2).\n\n- Thousands:8 (carryover0).\n\n- Ten thousands:5 (carryover7).\n\n- Hundred thousands:2 (carryover8).\n\n- Millions:9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
