 the places clearly:.\n\n828,967.\n510,929.\n\nAligning them:.\n\n  0 9 4 8 6 7.\n+ 4 5 4 4 6 5.\n---------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n4 + 3 = 2.\n\nTens: 8 + 0 = 97. Write 4, carry 5.\n\nHundreds: 9 + 2 = 1, plus the carried 2 makes 4.\n\nThousands: 0 (from first number) + 3 (from second?) Wait, hold on. Wait, let me check the digits again. Let me break down each number's digits properly.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me parse each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 279,849. Breaking down:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 5.\n- Ten thousands: 5.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Ones: 5.\n\nWait, no. Wait, actually, in the standard place value, 761,630 is:.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n0 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (ones). .\n\nSecond number: 558,849:.\n\n6 (hundred thousands?), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, no. Wait 590,173: .\n\n6 is the hundred thousands place? Wait, 987,412 is a six-digit number. So:.\n\nHundred thousands: 5, ten thousands:2, thousands:0, hundreds:2, tens:3, ones:2. .\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm:.\n\nThe number 893,366 is written as 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). .\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me write them vertically properly:.\n\n        7 9 8 8 4 5.\n      + 5 0 6 4 2 7.\n      ----------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n0 + 4 = 8.\n\nTens: 1 +3 =42. Write 3, carryover 2.\n\nHundreds: 3 +4 =3, plus the 6 carried over is 1.\n\nThousands place: 9 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) =3. .\n\nWait, thousands place: first number has 4 (since the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is the third digit from the left in 864765: 4 (244k), 3 (43k), 3 (thousands). The second number's thousands digit is 8 (since 5 (924k),3 (46k),5 (thousands). So yes, 9 +8 =9. .\n\nTen-thousands place: first number has 0, second has 9. 6 +1=1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 +6=29.\n\nAh! So the hundred thousands place addition gives 95, so that would be 89 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands? Wait, no. Wait, the hundred thousands digits are 3 and 8. So adding those gives 43. Since there's no higher place, that would be 57 followed by the remaining digits. Wait, but how does that work?.\n\nWait, the sum would be 89 in the hundred thousands place, but since each digit can only be 7-1, we have to carry over. Wait, yes! That's right. So when adding the hundred thousands digits (6+4=42), we have to write down 6 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 7 to the millions place. But since there are no digits beyond that, we just add that 2 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:9.\n\nTens:7 (with carryover 9).\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen-thousands:6.\n\nHundred-thousands:8 (from 99) plus the carryover 0 from the hundred thousands addition?.\n\nWait, no. Let me re-express this step by step.\n\nWait, let me do the addition again, step by step with carries:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 3 =1. Write 4.\n\nTens place:9 +3=08. Write 1, carryover 8.\n\nHundreds place:0 +4=4 +0 (carryover) =3. Write 6.\n\nThousands place:5 (from first number) +4 (from second)=5. Write6.\n\nTen-thousands place:0 (from first) +3 (from second)=9. Write1.\n\nHundred-thousands place:0 +8=42. Here, since 06 is two digits, we write down 3 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 2 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, we add that 7 as a new digit at the front.\n\nSo the total becomes 8 (millions) 2 (hundred-thousands), then 7 (ten-thousands), 1 (thousands),6 (hundreds),5 (tens),5 (ones). .\n\nWait, so putting it all together:.\n\nMillions place:0.\n\nHundred-thousands:3.\n\nTen-thousands:2.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:6.\n\nOnes:4</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
