ands place:** Finally, the hundred-thousands place. The digits here are 8 and 6. Adding them: 7 + 1 = 21. Then add the carried-over 5, resulting in 65. Since there are no more digits to the left, we just write down 89 here.\n\nPutting all the digits together, starting from the right: 7 (ones), 5 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 0 (ten-thousands), and 65 (hundred-thousands). Wait, actually, when writing the final number, the hundred-thousands place would be 04, but since we can't have a two-digit number in a single place, that means it's actually 7 in the millions place and 7 in the hundred-thousands. Oh right, I almost forgot! So the 64 in the hundred-thousands place actually becomes a 9 there and carry over 4 to the millions place. Let me correct that.\n\nLet me recalculate the hundred-thousands place properly. The digits there are 8 and 8, which add to 70, plus the carried-over 3 makes 86. So that's 8 in the hundred-thousands and carry over 9 to the millions place. Therefore, the millions place will have that 6. .\n\nPutting all together now:.\n\n- Millions place: 4.\n- Hundred-thousands: 1.\n- Ten-thousands: 6.\n- Thousands: 9.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 0.\n- Ones: 9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
