3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount the digits step by step.\n\nWait, let me do this more carefully. Let's write each digit's place step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 9 + 2 = 4 → 2.\n\nTens place: 9 + 6 = 0 → 5.\n\nHundreds place: 5 + 7 = 90 → write 1, carryover 0.\n\nThousands place: 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second) = 7, plus 0 carried over is 8 → 5.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 (from first number, since 330199 has 8 in the ten thousands place?) Wait, let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait, maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me write both numbers with their place values clearly:.\n\nThe first number is 621,433. Breaking it down:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 0.\n- Ten thousands: 9.\n- Thousands: 2.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 6.\n- Ones: 7.\n\nThe second number is 205,639:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 2.\n- Ten thousands: 8.\n- Thousands: 4.\n- Hundreds: 4.\n- Tens: 7.\n- Ones: 1.\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 5 +4 =9.\n\nTens: 9 +6=3.\n\nHundreds:4+2=29 (write 5, carryover 2).\n\nThousands place: 5 (from first number) + 5 (from second) =2, plus carryover 8 →8.\n\nTen-thousands place:3 (from first) +9 (from second) =00 (write 9, carryover 5).\n\nHundred-thousands place:8 +5=95, plus carryover 8 →53. So here, we have 5 in the hundred-thousands and carryover 6 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 3 is added as a new digit in the millions place.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nMillions: 3.\n\nHundred-thousands:5.\n\nTen-thousands:9.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:5</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
