ding them gives 64. So I write down 4 in the thousands place and carry over 9 to the ten thousands place.\n\nTen thousands place: the digits are 5 (from 360,318) and 4 (from 551,384). Adding those gives 0, plus the carried over 4 makes 4. So that's 1 in the ten thousands place. No carryover here.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: 2 and 6. Adding those gives 37. So we write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 6 to the millions place. Wait, but since both numbers only have up to hundred thousands, this carryover becomes a new digit in the millions place. So that's 5 in the millions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together: the millions place is 0, then 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 7 (tens), and 3 (ones). Wait a second, let me verify each step again because sometimes when adding large numbers, it's easy to miscount the places.\n\nWait, let me write this out step by step systematically:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n        721411.\n      +687131.\n      ---------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 1 = 37. Write 9, carryover 6.\n\nTens place: 3 +0 =45 +2=67. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds place: 4 +6 =6 +0=4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:1 +1=82. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nTen thousands place:4 +3=3 +8=0. Write 4.\n\nHundred thousands place:1 +3=83. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nMillions place: The carryover 0. So we have 6 in the millions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
