er 0 carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 9 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits are 3 (ones), 9 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 2 (thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), then 3 (hundred-thousands), and the carried over 3 in the millions place. Wait, let me check again because I might have messed up the places.\n\nWait a second, maybe I should do this step-by-step more carefully. Let me write down each digit's place properly:.\n\nLet me align them again:.\n\n\u2003\u20035 4 5 1 2 8.\n+ 1 1 4 7 6 6.\n--------------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 9 + 5 = 98 → 1, carryover 9.\n\nTens place: 0 + 8 = 43 + 3 = 39 → 2, carryover 9.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 8 = 1 + 4 (carryover) = 0 → 0, no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 5 + 5 = 0 → 4, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 0 = 72 → 2, carryover 4.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 + 5 = 79 + 5 (carryover) = 30 → so here, the 0 is written in the hundred-thousands place and another 6 is carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that becomes a 5 in the millions place.\n\nSo combining all digits from left to right: 8 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
